From 00b3f83e051b2837c9b47c7d2ad77e10190e627a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arbion Halili <a.halili@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:11:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] feat(castore/fs): serve indexed inodes from a shared
 index
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Teach SnixStoreFs to serve the inodes a shared index covers straight from
the mapping. `with_index` attaches one; `getattr`, `lookup`, `readdir`,
`readdirplus`, `open`, `readlink`, the extended attributes, the per-inode
DAX flag and the DAX `setupmapping` resolution all consult it first,
answering from the record — never the directory service — for an indexed
inode, and falling through to the existing tracker path otherwise.

The tracker stays, as an overlay for anything the index does not cover: a
root looked up that was not indexed, and its subtree. It is rebased to
allocate above the index's fixed inode range, so the two inode spaces
never collide, and it keeps the full forget/evict machinery for overlay
inodes.

Indexed inodes need none of that machinery, and keep no per-inode state
at all. Their metadata is immutable and shared between every daemon over
the one file, so there is nothing to evict and nothing a FORGET must draw
down: a FORGET for an indexed inode is a no-op, and the inode stays
resolvable however many times it is forgotten. Keeping no state is not
just an optimisation but the point — it is what lets the metadata be
read-only and shared. The mount root's listing, when an index is present,
is the index's roots table, so root readdir and lookup-by-basename hit the
mapping too; a basename the index does not cover still falls through to
the provider.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
